# Code Style and Formatting Rules

## TypeScript Guidelines

- Always use TypeScript strict mode
- Prefer `const` over `let`, avoid `var` completely
- Use async/await instead of promises when possible
- Avoid try-catch blocks unless absolutely necessary for error handling
- Write self-documenting code with clear variable names
- Keep functions small and focused on a single responsibility

## Naming Conventions

- Use camelCase for variables and functions
- Use PascalCase for classes and interfaces
- Use UPPER_CASE for constants
- Use kebab-case for file names
- Use descriptive names that explain intent

## Code Organization

- Keep imports organized and grouped
- Use barrel exports (index.ts) for clean imports
- Separate concerns into different modules
- Avoid deep nesting (max 3 levels)
- Use early returns to reduce nesting

## Comments and Documentation

- Add JSDoc comments for public APIs
- Use meaningful variable names instead of comments
- Document complex business logic
- Keep comments up to date with code changes
- Use TODO comments sparingly and with dates

## Formatting

- Use 2 spaces for indentation
- Use single quotes for strings
- Add trailing commas in objects and arrays
- Use semicolons consistently
- Limit line length to 80 characters

